5. What were AI winters?
An AI winter is a period when enthusiasm, funding, and research support for AI decline because expectations exceed the progress achieved.
AI experienced major periods of reduced support, notably in the 1970s and again during parts of the late 1980s and early 1990s.
Why did AI winters happen?
- Computers lacked sufficient processing power.
- Data was limited.
- Early systems struggled with complex real-world problems.
- Some AI promises were unrealistic.
- Research funding and commercial interest declined.
